<div class="wrap prose"><h1 class="ph">How we test</h1>
<p class="answer">Short version: we don't trust READMEs. Every night we take each add-on, run its real install command in every agent that supports it (Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Cursor, Pi), check the agent actually loaded it, and publish pass or fail with the reason. Latest run, {{ testing.runDate }}: {{ testing.addons }} add-ons, {{ testing.total }} installs, {{ testing.pass }} passed, {{ testing.fail }} failed.</p>
<h2>The 3 steps</h2>
<ol><li><b>We pull the add-ons from public lists.</b> The official Claude Code, Codex and Grok Build marketplaces, the official MCP reference servers, and MCP packages on npm. {{ skills.length }} listed today.</li>
<li><b>We run the real install, nightly.</b> Each agent's own CLI, inside a throwaway sandbox with an empty home folder and no accounts or API keys. Then the agent has to list it as installed, enabled or loaded. For MCP servers, the health check has to connect.</li>
<li><b>We publish pass/fail with reasons.</b> Every result on a skill's page has a date and a log excerpt. Failures stay up. Each skill page shows a "Last tested" date.</li></ol>
<h2>Latest run by agent</h2>
<div class="tw"><table class="matrix"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Installs</th><th>Passed</th><th>Failed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for a in site.agents %}{% set b = testing.by[a] or {} %}<tr><th scope="row">{{ a|agentName }}</th><td>{{ (b.pass or 0) + (b.fail or 0) }}</td><td class="tok">{{ b.pass or 0 }}</td><td>{{ b.fail or 0 }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Honest limits</h2><ul>
<li><b>Tested only where supported.</b> An add-on is only tested in agents that support that kind of add-on. That's why it's {{ testing.total }} tests across {{ testing.addons }} add-ons, not 5 × everything. Cursor's CLI has no plugin installer, so Cursor only gets MCP servers. MCP servers are tested in all 5 agents.</li>
<li><b>How each agent checks an MCP server.</b> Claude Code: <code>claude mcp list</code> must say Connected. Cursor: <code>cursor-agent mcp list</code> must say ready. Grok Build: <code>grok mcp doctor</code> must complete the handshake. Pi (0.99+ has built-in MCP): <code>pi mcp list</code> must say connected. Codex is the exception: its CLI has no MCP health check without signing in, so Codex must list the server as enabled, and then our harness does the MCP handshake (initialize + tools/list) with the exact command Codex would launch.</li>
<li><b>"Pass" means it installs and loads.</b> We don't run your prompts or poke every feature. A pass means you won't get stuck at step one.</li>
<li><b>No keys, no accounts.</b> Add-ons that need a login or API key often fail in our sandbox and may work fine once you add yours. The log tells you which.</li>
<li><b>Nights can get missed.</b> If a run fails or is skipped, the dates on the site say so. We never backfill a result we didn't run.</li></ul>
<h2>Why it matters</h2>
<p><b>For developers:</b> don't waste an hour on an install that was never going to work in your agent. Check the result, copy the command, move on.</p>
<p><b>For sponsors:</b> the people here are mid-decision, picking what to install in a specific agent today. A verified, high-intent audience, and sponsored slots are labeled and never change a test result. <a href="/advertise/">Advertise →</a></p>
